issue.description:
Correct the Workbench collapsible section triggers so they use the Body semantic typography role instead of the Heading role.

Observed issue:

* Collapsible trigger labels such as HEADING, BODY, and MONO are currently rendered with the Heading font family.
* These controls are Workbench chrome, not semantic content headings.
* This makes the trigger typography inconsistent with the accepted Theme v3 rule that ordinary Workbench UI should inherit/use Body typography by default.

Owner direction:

* Make collapsible trigger labels use the Body semantic typography role.
* Preserve the existing visual treatment otherwise.

Scope:

* Workbench collapsible trigger typography only.
* Apply consistently to equivalent collapsible section triggers across the Workbench where this shared pattern is used.

Preserve:

* current font size;
* current uppercase treatment;
* current weight unless the shared Body role already controls it by design;
* current tracking unless the shared Body role already controls it by design;
* current padding, border, radius, icon/chevron behavior, hover/focus/expanded states, and interaction semantics.

Do not:

* change the Heading/Body theme model;
* change semantic headings/titles elsewhere;
* change collapsible structure or behavior;
* add a one-off hard-coded font family;
* bypass the shared type-body semantic role.

Preferred implementation:

* use shared/root inheritance if the trigger should naturally inherit Body typography;
* otherwise apply the existing semantic type-body utility/class at the shared trigger component level;
* avoid duplicating styling across individual trigger instances.

Acceptance:

* HEADING, BODY, MONO, and equivalent Workbench collapsible trigger labels render with Body typography.
* Actual semantic headings/titles still use Heading typography.
* Changing the Body font family updates these trigger labels.
* No regressions to trigger layout, uppercase styling, chevron alignment, keyboard behavior, focus states, or accessibility.
* No unrelated typography changes.
